<%#
  ONE FOLDER BENEATH A FOLDER PANEL, AS ONE FLAT SECTION (stage 3, S3-1; flat since 2026-11-23).
  A working tree renders here too, entered rather than stopped at (Maker::ProjectFolderListing#tree),
  with a link to its commit and history pages.

  FLAT, HEADED BY ITS PATH BENEATH THE PANEL (2026-11-23, the owner's report: "organized non
  intuitively and the arrows are hard to follow"). This partial recursed: every subfolder nested one
  indent inside its parent with a disclosure arrow of its own, so the live `pc case`'s `out` panel
  stacked four levels of arrows and printed "svg (0)" over a folder whose files sat one level in.
  ProjectsHelper#folder_sections now hands the panel every folder that holds something, at one
  level, and this heading prints the folders between the panel and this one before its own name
  ("itx / four-piece / stl"), muted, so every arrow sits in one column and every heading says where
  its files are. It does not recurse, and must not: the sections beneath are already in the list.

  ALWAYS OPEN, WITH ONE EXCEPTION SINCE 2026-09-07. S2-2's "closed by default" rule belongs to the
  synthetic, cross-cutting Backups group alone (_attachment_group.html.erb, unchanged) — a REAL
  subfolder is not that, whatever a user happens to name it. A REPOSITORY is the exception, at the
  user's request: it renders CLOSED, and what is inside it is the Finder-style browser
  (projects/_repo_browser) rather than tiles and sections. The reason is what a repo IS — a tree a
  maker NAVIGATES, whose files are read and edited on their own pages — where a plain subfolder is a
  handful of things to see at a glance.

  THE COUNT IN THE SUMMARY IS THE WHOLE SUBTREE FOR A REPOSITORY AND THIS DIR'S OWN FILES FOR EVERY
  OTHER SECTION. A plain section shows exactly the tiles it renders and its subfolders carry their
  own counts in their own sections, so the direct-child number is the honest one there. A repository
  renders NO tiles at all — the browser inside it holds every file at every depth — so it counts its
  whole subtree (Maker::ProjectFolderListing::Dir#subtree_file_count).

  A SECTION IS A NESTED FOLDER ONLY, SINCE 2026-09-08. A DEPTH-ONE subfolder of the project's
  folder is a PANEL (projects/_folder_panel), which renders these.

  Locals: section (ProjectsHelper::FolderSection), view (one of Maker::Project::VIEWS) — the
  enclosing PANEL's view, passed down. It was `@view`, one page-wide cookie, until the view became
  per panel (spec decision 6): an ivar cannot say which panel is asking.
%>
<%# WHAT FILE_CAP LEFT OUT IS COUNTED (2026-11-12): a repository's browser lists every file, so its
    count is the real subtree; a plain section reads "(11 of 30)" when the cap drew 11 of its 30, and
    says where the rest are (2026-11-23), beside the enclosing panel's "Showing X of Y" line. %>
<% dir = section.dir %>
<% visible = dir.files %>
<% count = dir.repo? ? dir.subtree_file_count + dir.subtree_hidden_count : visible.size %>
<% count = "#{count} of #{count + dir.hidden_count}" if !dir.repo? && dir.hidden_count.positive? %>
<section class="attachment-group file-tree-section">
  <%# `open` on everything but a repository — see the header. %>
  <details<%= " open" unless dir.repo? %>>
    <%# THE NAME AND THE COUNT STAY ON ONE SOURCE LINE, WITH ONE LITERAL SPACE BETWEEN THEM —
        _attachment_group.html.erb's summary does the same, and for the same reason: a conditional
        badge/link block on its own line leaves a newline-plus-indentation TEXT NODE behind even
        when it renders nothing, which breaks a `text: /name \(N\)/` substring match against the
        rendered summary (found by driving a real request, not by reading the template). The trail
        is the same: one span per folder, each ending in its own " / ". %>
    <summary class="attachment-group-head"><% section.trail.each do |name| %><span class="section-trail"><%= name %> / </span><% end %><span class="section-name"><%= dir.name %></span><% if dir.repo? %> <span class="badge">repo</span><% end %> <span class="muted">(<%= count %>)</span><% if dir.repo? %> <%= link_to "Commits", repo_commit_path(dir.node_id), class: "linkbtn small", data: { turbo_prefetch: false } %> <%= link_to "History", repo_history_path(dir.node_id), class: "linkbtn small", data: { turbo_prefetch: false } %><% end %></summary>
    <%# THE CARD (stage 3, S3-2): README rendered, latest commit, current tag — at the top of the
        section, before its files. @repo_cards is keyed by node_id (ProjectsController#show) and
        holds one for every repo? Dir on the page, so a missing entry here would be a real bug, not
        a normal case — the `&&` guard is defensive rather than expected to ever fail. %>
    <% if dir.repo? %>
      <% if (card = @repo_cards[dir.node_id]) %>
        <%= render "projects/repo_card", card: card %>
      <% end %>
      <%# THE CARD STAYS ABOVE THE BROWSER and the browser replaces everything below it: no tiles,
          no rows, and no section for any folder inside it (ProjectsHelper#folder_sections stops at
          a repository). Listing them as well would draw every subdirectory twice — once as a
          section and once as a column. %>
      <%= render "projects/repo_browser", dir: %>
    <% else %>
      <%# As figures, the enclosing panel's stacks included (ProjectsHelper#stack_figures). %>
      <% figures = stack_figures(visible, folder_id: dir.node_id) %>
      <% if figures.any? %>
        <% if view == "list" %>
          <%= render "file_rows", rows: visible, figures: figures %>
        <% else %>
          <div class="attachments-grid">
            <% figures.each do |figure| %>
              <% if figure.is_a?(ProjectsHelper::StackFigure) %>
                <%= render "projects/stack_tile", figure: figure %>
              <% else %>
                <%= render "file_tile", row: figure %>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
      <% if dir.hidden_count.positive? %>
        <p class="muted section-more"><%= link_to "See all #{visible.size + dir.hidden_count} in the Library", files_path(folder: dir.node_id) %></p>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </details>
</section>
